    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 061622\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n922 AM PDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through Friday, with \n  dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Our next system brings cooler temperatures, gusty winds and\n  precipitation chances this weekend into next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\n\nBroad ridging remains in place through the work week, keeping \ntemperatures well above normal (6-12 degrees) for early October.\nThe heat will be most pronounced in Death Valley National Park, \nwhere highs near 110 and lows in the 80s yield major to extreme \nHeatRisk. An Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Friday\nevening for elevations below 2000' in the park. Elsewhere, minor \nto moderate HeatRisk is anticipated with the above normal \ntemperatures. There are low chances (20%) for isolated showers and\na few rumbles of thunder in Lincoln County this afternoon, but \notherwise dry conditions persist.\n\nThe forecast becomes rather complicated over the weekend and into \nearly next week as multiple features interact with each other and \ninfluence weather across the western US. The three features of\nnote are a shortwave coming out of western Canada, a cutoff low \noff the CA coast, and Hurricane Rachel. Confidence is pretty high \nin widespread gusty winds and cooler temperatures beginning \nSaturday. The main uncertainty remains on the precipitation side. \nLatest guidance has trended wetter, particularly the eastern half \nof the CWA. The NBM and raw ensemble guidance have 20-50% chances \nof 0.50\"+ generally over the Spring Mountains and points east \n(chances increasing with eastward extent) during the Sunday-Monday\ntimeframe. Odds of 1\"+ are 10-30% over the same area and time. \nDon't want to get too far ahead of ourselves, as predicting the \ninteraction of the 3 features at this range is a tall task and \nsmall changes will make a big difference in precipitation \npotential. However, this system and forecast trends will certainly\nbe something to watch, so stay tuned.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Light \nwinds follow typical daily directional trends with occasional \nvariability through the forecast period. FEW mid level clouds \ndevelop today and tomorrow in the afternoon.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds follow typical \ndaily directional patterns with speeds between 5 and 10 knots for \nmost locations over the next 24 hours. A few rain showers may \ndevelop in Lincoln County during the afternoon. Otherwise, expect \nmostly clear skies with FEW mid level clouds. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           TUE, OCT 6  WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     100(2024)   99(1978)*   98(2024)*   97(2024)*\nBishop        94(2024)    94(1965)*   95(1996)    93(2015)\nNeedles       111(2024)   107(2024)   107(2024)   104(1996)*\nDaggett       105(2024)   102(2024)   101(1996)*  103(1980)\nKingman       100(2024)   99(2024)    98(1996)    98(1910)\nDesert Rock   100(2024)   99(2024)    98(1996)*   97(1996)\nDeath Valley  110(2024)*  109(2024)*  112(1996)*  112(1996)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      TUE, OCT 6  WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     74(2024)*   75(2024)*   76(2024)*   72(2024)*\nBishop        52(2018)    57(1943)    51(1960)    61(2025)\nNeedles       77(2023)*   76(1947)*   76(2024)*   76(1988)*\nDaggett       75(2024)    72(2024)*   69(1980)*   69(1996)*\nKingman       67(1949)*   69(1947)*   67(1960)*   68(1926)*\nDesert Rock   66(1990)*   65(1980)*   70(2024)    65(1991)*\nDeath Valley  83(1943)*   80(1943)*   85(1964)*   78(2024)*\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION/CLIMATE...Woods\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
